  "answer_text": "That whitish film is very common, and depending on what it feels like, it is almost certainly caused by one of two things: **mineral deposits (limescale)** or a **biological biofilm**. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what both are, why your fridge filter isn't stopping them, and how to tell them apart.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Mineral Deposits (Limescale)\n* **What it is:** A buildup of minerals, primarily calcium and magnesium.\n* **Why your filter doesn't stop it:** Standard refrigerator water filters (usually activated carbon filters) are excellent at removing chlorine, bad tastes, odors, lead, and organic compounds. However, they **do not** remove dissolved minerals. To remove minerals, you would need a specialized water softener or a reverse osmosis system.\n* **How it forms:** Every time you take a sip or refill the glass, a thin layer of water clings to the glass walls. As that water evaporates into the room, the pure H\u2082O escapes into the air, but the microscopic minerals are left behind. Over the course of a week, these layers stack up, forming a visible, hazy, white chalky film.\n* **What it feels like:** When dry, it feels slightly rough, dusty, or chalky to the touch. \n\n### 2. Biofilm (Bacterial/Yeast Colony)\n* **What it is:** A microscopic layer of bacteria, yeast, and fungi bound together by a sticky substance they secrete. \n* **How it forms:** Every time your lips touch the glass, you transfer saliva, skin cells, and natural mouth bacteria into the cup. Because the glass contains water and is left out at room temperature, it becomes the perfect petri dish. Even though the water starts clean, the introduced bacteria will colonize the glass, multiplying over a week to form a visible, cloudy, translucent-white film. \n* **What it feels like:** When the glass is wet, a biofilm will feel **slimy, slippery, or greasy** to the touch. \n\n---\n\n### How to Tell Which One It Is\nThe easiest test is to touch the film when the glass is wet:\n* If it is **slimy or slippery**, it is a **biofilm**.\n* If it is **scratchy, rough, or does not budge** when you rub it with your finger under running water, it is **mineral buildup**. \n\n*(Note: It is also highly common for the film to be a combination of both\u2014bacteria love to anchor themselves to the rough, microscopic surfaces of mineral deposits!)*\n\n---\n\n### How to Clean and Prevent It\n\n* **If it's Minerals:** Because minerals are alkaline, they easily dissolve in acid. Fill the glass with **white vinegar** or a mixture of warm water and lemon juice, and let it soak for 10\u201315 minutes. The film will wipe away effortlessly with no scrubbing required. To prevent this, dry your glass with a towel instead of letting it air-dry.\n* **If it's Biofilm:** Wash the glass with **hot, soapy water and a sponge**. Simply rinsing the glass with water will not break down a biofilm. \n\n**The Best Practice:**\nTo keep things sanitary, try not to use the same glass for more than **2 to 3 days** without giving it a proper wash with dish soap. Even though \"nothing but water\" has gone into it, the biological bacteria from your mouth will inevitably take over after a week.",
